{"resourceIdentifier":"3343","resourceName":"XiCO2e: Mexican Peninsula Forest Project","description":"The XiCO2e: Mexican Peninsula Forest Project aims to increase carbon removal through Improved Forest Management activities in communal-land properties (ejidos) located in the Yucatán Peninsula in the southeastern state of Quintana Roo, Mexico. The project is developed under the Verified Carbon Standard (VCS) methodology VM0003 v1.3 - Methodology for Improved Forest Management Through Extension of Rotation Age (IFM ERA).\r\nThe Project Area covers 70,246 hectares and is comprised of semi-evergreen tropical forest with broad species diversity, having as its primary target commercial species such as mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla), Spanish cedar (Cedrela odorata), Sapotilla (Manilkara zapota), Fiddlewood (Vitex gaumeri), Tsalam (Lysiloma bahamensis), Ramon (Brosimum alicastrum), Katalox (Swartzia cubensis), Shaving bush tree (Pseudobombax ellipticum), Copperwood (Bursera simaruba), and Chechen (Metopium brownei), among several other tropical commercial species.\r\nThe common practice of harvesting in the ejidos consists of selection cuts of tropical species in management units during the established rotation period. The project will extend the rotation cycle by +5, +4, and +2 years. Additionally, the project aims to implement a monitoring procedure to systematize and centralize information related to forest practices. The project is expected to remove 4,599,024 tonnes of CO2e over 30 years, with an annual average of 170,334 tonnes of CO2e.","location":{"latitude":19.131400,"longitude":-88.314816},"inPublicCommentPeriod":false,"lastPublicCommentPeriod":{"startInclusive":"2023-04-12","endExclusive":"2023-05-13","endInclusive":"2023-05-12"},"attributes":[{"code":"PROJECT_ID","values":[{"type":"string","value":"3343"}]},{"code":"STATE_PROVINCE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Campeche & Quintana Roo states"}]}],"participationSummaries":[{"programCode":"VCS","attribute